WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.514
Picture the payments app on a busy Saturday.

2
00:00:05.514 --> 00:00:12.356
Every card payment goes into Kafka, a program that keeps a log of messages and lets other programs read it.

3
00:00:12.533 --> 00:00:18.108
A second program, the ledger, reads each payment and adds it to the right account's balance.

4
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.897
At 11:02 the ledger crashes and restarts by itself.

5
00:00:21.897 --> 00:00:26.975
By Monday, one account is 20.00 too high: two payments were added twice.

6
00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:33.171
Another team ran the same program with two lines swapped, and lost three payments instead.

7
00:00:33.171 --> 00:00:34.239
Which two lines?

8
00:00:34.239 --> 00:00:35.507
Hold that question.

9
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.617
The ledger's reader is a consumer, and it runs a loop.

10
00:00:39.617 --> 00:00:42.663
Poll: ask Kafka for the next few payments.

11
00:00:42.663 --> 00:00:44.258
Process: add each one.

12
00:00:44.258 --> 00:00:46.362
Commit: save how far you got.

13
00:00:46.362 --> 00:00:46.869
Repeat.

14
00:00:47.033 --> 00:00:49.988
A restart doesn't carry on where the crash stopped.

15
00:00:49.988 --> 00:00:51.668
Everything in memory is gone.

16
00:00:51.668 --> 00:00:54.449
It carries on from the last place it wrote down.

17
00:00:54.449 --> 00:00:56.767
So when should it write that place down?

18
00:00:56.967 --> 00:00:59.626
Think of reading a long book over many evenings.

19
00:00:59.626 --> 00:01:02.174
Your finger marks the line you're reading now.

20
00:01:02.174 --> 00:01:04.667
A bookmark marks where you'll start tomorrow.

21
00:01:04.867 --> 00:01:08.927
If you nod off, your finger slips, and only the bookmark is left.

22
00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:13.947
In Kafka, the finger is the position: the next offset the consumer will read.

23
00:01:13.947 --> 00:01:15.143
It lives in memory.

24
00:01:15.143 --> 00:01:17.472
The bookmark is the committed offset.

25
00:01:17.472 --> 00:01:21.500
Kafka saves it for the group, and it moves only when you commit.

26
00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:22.621
Let's run it.

27
00:01:22.621 --> 00:01:25.632
Consumer 1 polls and gets offsets 0 to 4.

28
00:01:25.632 --> 00:01:30.479
The position jumps to 5 at once, before a single payment is added.

29
00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:32.829
The committed offset is still 0.

30
00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:35.789
It adds pay-00 to pay-04 to the ledger.

31
00:01:35.789 --> 00:01:38.828
Then it commits 5: the next record to read.

32
00:01:39.133 --> 00:01:42.930
Next poll: 5 to 9, and the position moves to 10.

33
00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:47.360
It adds pay-05 and pay-06, and dies while adding pay-07.

34
00:01:47.567 --> 00:01:50.963
Consumer 2, a new copy of the program, takes over.

35
00:01:50.963 --> 00:01:54.223
Where does it start, and what happens to pay-05?

36
00:01:54.223 --> 00:01:55.309
Pause and guess.

37
00:01:58.567 --> 00:02:02.181
It knows only what Kafka saved: committed offset 5.

38
00:02:02.181 --> 00:02:06.857
So pay-05 and pay-06, already in the ledger, arrive a second time.

39
00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.477
Then the rest, and a commit of 10.

40
00:02:09.477 --> 00:02:11.364
The balance ends at 120.00.

41
00:02:11.364 --> 00:02:14.160
That's the Saturday bug: 20.00 too high.

42
00:02:14.433 --> 00:02:19.965
Process, then commit, means every payment is processed at least once, and some twice.

43
00:02:19.965 --> 00:02:21.657
It's called at-least-once.

44
00:02:21.867 --> 00:02:26.061
So the other team thought: commit first, then nothing can ever repeat.

45
00:02:26.061 --> 00:02:29.176
Let's be fair and replay the second batch their way.

46
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.213
Consumer 1 polls 5 to 9 and commits 10 at once.

47
00:02:33.213 --> 00:02:36.944
It adds pay-05 and pay-06, and dies at pay-07.

48
00:02:37.167 --> 00:02:38.905
Consumer 2 starts at 10.

49
00:02:38.905 --> 00:02:44.988
pay-07, pay-08 and pay-09 are never added, and nothing will ever deliver them again.

50
00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:49.723
It's like moving your bookmark to the end of the chapter before you read it.

51
00:02:49.723 --> 00:02:54.459
Nod off, and tomorrow you skip three pages, with no idea what happened in them.

52
00:02:54.459 --> 00:02:58.776
That's at-most-once: each record processed at most once, and some never.

53
00:02:58.967 --> 00:03:02.561
For payments, at-least-once is the only safe choice.

54
00:03:02.561 --> 00:03:06.224
A lost payment is a missing line that nobody notices.

55
00:03:06.224 --> 00:03:07.883
A repeat can be spotted.

56
00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:08.616
Watch.

57
00:03:08.616 --> 00:03:16.270
Consumer 2 starts at 5 again, and the ledger skips any payment id it has already applied.

58
00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:18.593
pay-05 and pay-06: skipped.

59
00:03:18.593 --> 00:03:19.969
The rest: added.

60
00:03:19.969 --> 00:03:20.571
100.00.

61
00:03:21.867 --> 00:03:22.511
Two notes.

62
00:03:22.511 --> 00:03:27.347
By default, the consumer commits by itself every five seconds, inside poll.

63
00:03:27.347 --> 00:03:31.989
That's at-least-once only if you finish each batch before the next poll.

64
00:03:31.989 --> 00:03:37.211
Hand the work to helper threads, and it can commit payments nobody has added yet.

65
00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:38.531
And exactly once?

66
00:03:38.531 --> 00:03:43.655
The commit goes to Kafka, but the work lands somewhere else, like a database.

67
00:03:43.655 --> 00:03:47.515
No order of two writes to two systems makes them one step.

68
00:03:47.515 --> 00:03:52.040
At-least-once, plus work that ignores repeats, gets the same result.

69
00:03:52.233 --> 00:03:59.493
In Java and Kotlin: turn auto-commit off, then in the loop, poll, do the work, and only then call commitSync.

70
00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:01.461
So which two lines?

71
00:04:01.461 --> 00:04:02.722
Process and commit.

72
00:04:02.722 --> 00:04:06.439
Both teams had the same two lines, in a different order.

73
00:04:06.439 --> 00:04:13.475
Move the bookmark after the work, give every payment an id, and a crash costs only a little reading twice.

74
00:04:14.033 --> 00:04:15.098
One quick check.

75
00:04:15.098 --> 00:04:20.020
You pause a podcast, close the app at once, and open it on another device.

76
00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:23.612
It starts a few seconds early, so you hear them twice.

77
00:04:23.612 --> 00:04:25.674
At-least-once, or at-most-once?

78
00:04:25.674 --> 00:04:26.739
Pause and think.

79
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:30.879
At-least-once.

80
00:04:30.879 --> 00:04:36.968
Its bookmark was saved a little behind where you were, so you hear a bit twice, and miss nothing.
